note right of "ores::synthetic::messaging::list_folders_request"
Every request struct carries a using response_type and a static nats_subject;
the automated pass skips both, because it skips using and static lines. The
per-entity families have nine requests each: list, get, get_many, put,
put_many, delete, delete_many, list_versions, get_version. Each of the nine
also declares static requires_session, because an operation that produces a
session cannot present one. Subjects read synthetic.v1.<resource>.<verb>, and
versions use synthetic.v1.<resource>_versions.<verb>.
end note
note bottom of "ores::synthetic::messaging::get_many_ir_curve_generation_config_process_parameter_values_request"
The five "<name> : struct" rows in this request family are an artifact of the
automated pass, not data members. They come from a using response_type
declaration that wraps onto its continuation line, which then reads as a
field. The code declares no such member. The affected boxes are the get_many,
put_many, delete_many, list_versions, and get_version requests for
ir_curve_generation_config_process_parameter_value.
end note
note right of "ores::synthetic::messaging::list_feeds_request"
Feed control is kind-agnostic: one start, stop, and list shape for every asset
class, keyed by config_id. The server resolves the config, its children, and
the refdata context from that id and builds the feed through the producer
factory, so a client never names a source_name and never supplies producer
parameters.
end note
note right of "ores::synthetic::messaging::preview_ir_curve_shape_request"
Stateless dry run. It derives a rate per template row from the process's
current state, with no ticking and nothing persisted or published, so an
editor can show the curve a save would produce.
preview_ir_curve_template_row is deliberately not the persisted
ir_curve_template_entry: a row being edited has no id, party, or tenant yet.
The handler shares price_ir_curve_entry() with the live feed.
end note
note right of "ores::synthetic::messaging::simulate_ir_curve_paths_request"
Stateless dry run, the IR analogue of simulate_fx_spot_paths_request. It
builds the real short-rate process and returns the whole batch in one
response. parameter_spec is the wire-shaped analogue of one
{parameter_definition_id, value} row: the editor carries the parameter name
itself, and the handler re-materialises the catalogue entry on the fly.
end note
note right of "ores::synthetic::messaging::generate_organisation_request"
The one request whose response carries counts rather than rows. It returns the
seed actually used, so a later request with that seed reproduces the same
organisation exactly.
end note

